# ADR-0023: Select TDD source currency by verified terminal evidence order

Status: accepted

## Context

TDD requirement coverage is intentionally restricted to the active CHANGE and
generation. Source-currency validation has a different purpose: it determines
whether the current test source still matches the newest trustworthy passing
fingerprint. The existing validator reused the active-cycle predicate and then
overwrote a map in evidence-array order. In repositories containing legacy
unscoped cycles, that can select an older fingerprint while excluding a newer
scoped Green cycle owned by a completed CHANGE, producing false
`TDD_TEST_STALE` diagnostics that cannot be repaired with `tdd migrate` or
`tdd void`.

## Decision

Separate source-currency selection from requirement-coverage selection. For
each test, derive eligible terminal fingerprint evidence from phases whose
payload, hash-chain predecessor, and monotonic order linkage are verified.
Red and Green are required, their verified orders must satisfy
`red.order < green.order`, and source-currency eligibility does not require an
Implementation checkpoint. A linked valid Refactor or a linked human-approved
migration may provide the terminal fingerprint when it has the greatest order.

While a CHANGE is active, define the active scope as cycles whose missing
generation is interpreted as generation 1, whose effective generation equals
the active generation, and whose `changeId` is absent or equals the active
CHANGE ID. Stale-check membership adds valid Green evidence, while work-event
and void operation scoping use the active scope without requiring Green.
When a CHANGE is selected but has no active generation, validation retains its
existing `CHANGE_GENERATION_PHASE` exit-2 failure, while migration and void
preserve maintenance behavior by treating the state as no active operation
scope.
Membership uses persisted ownership at evaluation time, including ownership
adopted by later normal phase recording. Without an active CHANGE, target every
test ID with valid Green evidence. For each target, resolve a readonly
`{ cycle, order, fingerprint }` selection across all CHANGE scopes and
generations by verified order, never by array position. The validator compares
that returned fingerprint outside its active-cycle coverage loop and never
re-sorts raw phase fields. A valid void participates as an event: when it is the
greatest verified event for the test, select only the greatest eligible
terminal fingerprint strictly before that void. Otherwise select the greatest
eligible terminal fingerprint. Keep active CHANGE and generation filtering
unchanged for coverage and release credit.

Build the cycle grouping and chain-record indexes once per validation. Each
chain-index entry retains the record and its chain position; derive predecessor
identity from the preceding chain record and duplicate multiplicity from the
matching-entry count. Memoize source text by trace path and fingerprints by test
identity. Migration always reuses
the same greatest verified event and selector as validation and, while a CHANGE
is active, restricts target, void, and work candidates to the active scope.
Cycle-ID-less or chain-unverified records cannot become eligible migration
terminals and therefore use the retained no-valid-Green error rather than the
legacy missing-cycle-ID guard. After verified selection, any persisted
migration record, valid or malformed, uses the retained already-migrated
exit-2 error and is never overwritten; malformed migration evidence requires a
new Red/Green cycle to establish later current evidence. Void recording uses
the same index and active-scope rule for both target events and eligible
fallback candidates, requiring the fallback strictly before the greatest
verified work-event order, whether that event is a valid Red without Green or a
linked non-passing Green. Without an active CHANGE, both target and fallback
may consider all cycles for the test. A missing linked target in the operation
scope or a target cycle bearing an unverified void returns
`{ voided: false, reason: "<testId> has no verifiable dangling TDD cycle in the current operation scope." }`
with exit code 1 and never overwrites the existing void evidence.
Void targeting compares terminal fingerprint evidence, valid voids, and work
events through the shared greatest-event index. A terminal event supplied by
Green, Refactor, or approved migration is classified by its cycle's valid Green
and uses the retained valid-Green rejection; a valid void uses the retained
already-voided rejection. A greatest valid-Red or linked non-passing-Green work
event remains a voidable dangling target when its cycle has no valid Green and
no persisted void record. Before this verified-event selection, apply the
array-last missing-cycle-ID and missing-chain structural guards only within the
operation scope, including the missing-chain “another cycle exists” predicate,
so their historical exit-2 messages retain precedence over valid-Green and
already-voided messages without granting array position chronology.

## Alternatives considered

- Keep using the active-cycle map for staleness. Rejected because it excludes
  newer completed-CHANGE fingerprints while admitting legacy unscoped cycles.
- Treat evidence-array position as chronology. Rejected because authoritative
  chronology is the verified monotonic order log, and projections or merges can
  expose records in a misleading array order.
- Exclude every cycle whose `changeId` is absent from currency checks. Rejected
  because modern unscoped evidence can still have verifiable cycle identity,
  hash-chain linkage, and monotonic order; absence of a CHANGE owner alone does
  not prove that its fingerprint is stale. Pre-order-log legacy evidence remains
  ineligible because it cannot establish that linkage.
- Require operators to migrate or void the selected legacy cycle. Rejected
  because migration correctly refuses real fingerprint differences, void
  targets the actual latest cycle, and neither command repairs a selector bug.
- Re-record every historical test through a fresh Red/Green cycle. Rejected
  because it fabricates unrelated CHANGE work and is unnecessary when newer
  verified evidence already exists.

## Consequences

- Source-currency checks can consult foreign and prior-generation cycles, but
  those cycles remain unable to satisfy active coverage or release evidence.
- Modern unscoped cycles in the active generation retain their existing ability
  to establish source-currency target membership and coverage candidacy; this
  change does not widen or remove that legacy compatibility behavior.
- Red-only and dangling void-only active cycles do not establish new stale-check
  membership from foreign evidence; an earlier valid Green can still establish
  membership for a void fallback.
- Malformed Green, Refactor, migration, or void evidence cannot gain selector
  priority merely through a large unverified order value.
- Valid migration ordering becomes part of source-currency selection and
  therefore requires both linkage verification and a recorded human approver.
- A valid latest void creates a strict historical boundary; a later verified
  Green or terminal fingerprint becomes current normally.
- The selection helper is shared by ordinary staleness and void fallback,
  eliminating two competing notions of the effective latest cycle.
- Fingerprint migration uses the same effective chronology instead of
  evidence-array position.
- Migration retains `No TDD cycle found for <testId>.` when no cycle exists in
  any scope and uses the retained `<testId> has no valid Green phase to
  migrate.` exit-2 error when cycles exist only outside the active operation
  scope or qualifying work is greatest.
- Active-scope migration does not mutate or use a foreign void candidate, so a
  successful active migration can remain globally non-current when
  repository-wide validation is bounded by a later valid foreign or
  prior-generation void; completing or abandoning the active CHANGE enables
  repository-wide repair.
- An open verified Red is an explicit event that suppresses stale diagnostics
  during the Red-to-Green interval.
- Void admission uses verified event order and linkage instead of array slices.
- With an active CHANGE, open or non-passing work suppresses staleness only in
  the target's active membership scope. Without an active CHANGE, unscoped work
  may suppress any terminal while scoped work must match the unbounded greatest
  terminal selection's CHANGE/generation scope; that scope is fixed before a
  void bound is applied so a void cannot retroactively change which work was in
  progress relative to the current terminal history. When the unbounded terminal
  is unscoped, only unscoped work qualifies.
- Existing array-position supersession classification is out of scope and does
  not participate in source-currency selection.
- Void's array-last structural guards apply only within the operation's active
  candidate scope and preserve malformed-evidence messages without granting
  chronology or fallback priority.
- Public CLI shape, established exit classes for retained conditions, help
  text, and the no-fallback reason string remain unchanged. Order-based
  applicability of retained messages, foreign-only active-scope exit-1
  rejection, trimmed migration-approver rejection and diagnostic reuse, and
  active-scope restrictions for migration and void are registered intentional
  behavioral differences under REQ-M5-COMPAT-013.

